{
  "description": "#4667 — READ-command differential over `get_backlinks`, the one of the three backlink candidates the #3347 allowlist labelled 'fixture candidate: not written yet' that the row projection can express (the two GROUPED siblings answer under `groups[].blocks`; see their allowlist entries). The backend reads the materialized `block_links` table and answers `ORDER BY bl.source_id ASC` under an `{id}` keyset (`agaric-store/src/pagination/links.rs`); the mock rescans block CONTENT for `[[ULID]]` tokens (`handlers/links.ts`). Three decisions have to agree for the two to land on the same page: which edges exist (S5 linked and then stopped, so a stale edge set serves it), the source-id ordering, and the `limit` + cursor keyset. Every linking block is a SEED block edited by an op, so its id is byte-identical on both stacks and the sort order is comparable; the link TOKENS arrive via `edit_block` because the seed insert bypasses the materializer and would leave `block_links` empty on the backend. NOT pinned, and deliberately: (a) the space filter — the harness has one space, so `active` and `global` select the same rows and `backlinks_global_scope` pins only that `Global` is served unscoped rather than refused the way `list_trash` refuses it; and (b) nothing else — the `b.deleted_at IS NULL` join filter was the other gap and #4848 closed it. B8 is the state it needs: a block that links to B1 and is then soft-deleted. `DeleteBlock` enqueues no `ReindexBlockLinks` (`src/reconciliation_oracle.rs`), so its `block_links` row survives the tombstone by design — it is in `page_links` below — and every reader drops it at query time instead. `backlinks_all_in_source_id_order` is where that is asserted: B8 sorts last, so a `get_backlinks` that skipped the join filter would serve it as a fourth source. The mock used to skip deleted sources inside `deriveLinkEdges`, which made the SNAPSHOT leg red on this exact state; it now mirrors the table, with the count-time `src.deleted_at IS NULL` moved to `pageLinkStats` (`handlers/shared.ts`), the one place both counting consumers reach it through. #4853 is the mirror image: B10 links to page B9, then B9 — the TARGET — is soft-deleted, and the surviving `B10 -> B9` row in `page_links` below is what pins it. B10 and B9 are new seeds rather than a second token on an existing pair, so the surviving edge is interesting for exactly one reason and none of the `get_backlinks` steps above move. Derivation in `session-1602`.",
